arXiv:2209.06047v2 Announce Type: replace Abstract: We present a simple algorithm for the calculation of event weights embedding the effects of anomalous electric and magnetic dipole moments in simulation of $e^-e^+	o 	au^-	au^+ (n\gamma)$ events, and the subsequent decay of the $	au$ leptons produced. The impact of these weights on the spin-correlation matrix and the total cross section is taken into account. The algorithm is prepared to work in situ the Monte Carlo KKMC program without the need for introducing any external change to the generator libraries. As an example, $e^-e^+ 	o 	au^-	au^+ (n\gamma), \; 	au^- 	o ho^- 
u_	au 	o \pi^- \pi^0 
u_	au, \ 	au^+ 	o ho^+  ar{
u}_	au 	o \pi^+ \pi^0  ar{
u}_	au$ events were simulated at a center-of-mass energy of 10.58 GeV. The distributions of the acoplanarity angle between the planes spanned by the $\pi^- \pi^0$ and the $\pi^+ \pi^0$ momenta of, respectively, $ho^-$ and $ho^+$ decays and in the rest frame of the entirely visible $ho^-ho^+$ system are presented for different values of the coupling constants incorporating anomalous electric and magnetic dipole moments in the $	au^- 	au^+ \gamma$ vertex.

arXiv:2606.05061v2 Announce Type: replace Abstract: We study the angular distribution of the Higgs boson decay $h	o\ell ar{
u}_\ell ar{\ell}^\prime
u_{\ell^\prime}$ with $\ell
eq\ell^\prime$. Due to the presence of two undetected neutrinos, a complete angular analysis is not experimentally feasible. To overcome this, we reorganize the kinematics from the conventional lepton-neutrino pairs into a charged-lepton pair and a neutrino pair, i.e.~$\ell ar{\ell}^\prime$ and $ ar{
u}_\ell
u_{\ell^\prime}$. This allows us to express the differential decay rate in terms of experimentally accessible variables, including the invariant mass squared of the neutrino pair. Using the effective field theory framework, we derive this rate and integrate over the neutrino-associated angles. This parametrization provides a clean and measurable angular distribution, offering a new probe of the $hWW$ coupling and possible beyond-the-Standard-Model contributions.
